          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmology
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle+physics"} particle physics
            %em baryogenesis
            one refers to the process by which an abundance of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryons"} baryons
            is produced in the early
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
            seen all around. Since in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antimatter"} antimatter
            are
            %em essentially
            always produced symmetrically (“conservation of baryon number”), the topic of baryogenesis is to a large extent the study of how the matter/antimatter symmetry may be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/broken+symmetry"} broken
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Sakharov67"}> Sakharov 67
            ) formulated three conditions jointly necessary for matter/antimatter asymmetry leading to baryogenesis in the early universe, now known as the
            %em Sakharov conditions
          %ol
            %li
              %p violation of baryon number conservation;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CP+problem"} C and CP violation
            %li
              %p departure from thermal equilibrium.
          %p
            Indeed, by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} quantum anomaly
            in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axial+anomaly"} axial anomaly
            (a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chiral+anomaly"} chiral anomaly
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Jackiw08"} Jackiw 08
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/divergence"} divergence
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryon+current"} baryon current
            (the chiral version of the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+current"} Dirac current
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Hooft76"} t’Hooft 76
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi μ
                  %msubsup
                    %mi j
                    %mi b
                    %mi μ
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi μ
                  %msubsup
                    %mi j
                    %mi L
                    %mi μ
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mi f
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi g
                          %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mn 32
                        %msup
                          %mi π
                          %mn 2
                    %msubsup
                      %mi W
                      %mrow
                        %mi μ
                        %mi ν
                      %mi α
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi W
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                        %mi μ
                        %mi ν
                    %mo −
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mrow
                            %mi g
                            %mo ′
                          %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mn 32
                        %msup
                          %mi π
                          %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mrow
                        %mi μ
                        %mi ν
                        %mi ρ
                        %mi σ
                    %msup
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %mi μ
                        %mi ν
                    %msup
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %mi ρ
                        %mi σ
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\partial_\mu j_b^\mu
                  \=
                  \\partial_\mu j_L^\mu
                  \=
                  n_f
                  \\left(
                  \\frac{g^2}{32 \pi^2}
                  W_{\mu \nu}^\alpha \tilde W^{\alpha \mu \nu}
                  \-
                  \\frac{{g'}^2}{32\pi^2} \epsilon_{\mu \nu \rho \sigma} F^{\mu \nu} F^{\rho \sigma}
                  \\right)
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\nabla
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electroweak+field"} electroweak field
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            of a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SU
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(2)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connection"} principal connection
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
          %p
            (see e.g.
            %a{:href => "#Dine90"} Dine 90, (4)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#TroddenCarroll"} Trodden-Carroll, 4.9.3
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Shu08"} Shu 08, (7.6)
          %p In more intrinsic notation and ignoring numerical constants, the statement is that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %mo ⋆
                  %msub
                    %mi j
                    %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{d} \star j_b = \langle F_\nabla \wedge F_\nabla \rangle
                  \\,.
          %p
            Properly interpreted (see at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "Yang-Mills+instanton#FromTheMathsToThePhysicsStory"} Yang-Mills instanton – from the correct maths to the traditional physics story
            %em local
            expression for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+3-bundle"} 3-connection
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+line+3-bundle"} Chern-Simons line 3-bundle
            associated with the gauge field. If the latter is globally nontrivial in that it is in an
            = succeed "-sector" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton"} instanton
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+Chern+class"} second Chern class
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi F
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle F\wedge F\rangle
            over closed manifolds is an integer – the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton"} instanton
            number” – and in conclusion there is baryon generation proportional to this number (physics lingo also:
            = succeed ")." do
              %em sphaleron transitions
          %p
            Notice that this would mean that baryons “are” a kind of topological twist, different from, but not entirely unlike to the old idea of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+Vortex+Atoms"} On Vortex Atoms
            %em
              %a{:href => "On+Vortex+Atoms#SimilarityToParticlePhysics"} Similarlity to concepts of modern particle physics
          %p
            That this process was at least one source of baryogenesis in the early universe is plausible but not certain. The process is heavily suppressed by inverse energy/temperature (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Dine90"} Dine 90, around (9)
          %h2#Exposition Exposition
          %p
            One needs to be careful that the concept of “particle” itself is only well defined on Minkowski spacetime, hence not on cosmological scales. But nevertheless there is a fermion current 3-form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi Σ
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Σ
                  %mi J
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Q_\Sigma \coloneqq   \int_\Sigma J \in \mathbb{R}
          %p
            has the interpretation of the total net
            %em charge
            carried by the stuff measured by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
          %p
            This is familiar from, say, Maxwell’s equations, where such a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            appears on the right hand side as the electromagnetic source form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mi J
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d  \star F = J
                  \\,.
          %p
            Now given any such current 3-form, then its de Rham differential
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d J
            measures how the charges
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            changes as time progresses. Because let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_2
            be two spatial slices of spacetimes, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_4
            a piece of spacetime connecting them, i.e. such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 4
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\partial X_4 = \Sigma_2 - \Sigma_1
                  \\,.
          %p
            Then
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stokes%27s+theorem"} Stokes's theorem
            gives that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d J
            is the local change of charge, because:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi Q
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi Σ
                                %mn 2
                          %mo −
                          %msub
                            %mi Q
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi Σ
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi Σ
                                %mn 2
                          %mi J
                          %mo −
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi Σ
                                %mn 1
                          %mi J
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mrow
                              %mo ∂
                              %msub
                                %mi X
                                %mn 4
                          %mi J
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi X
                                %mn 4
                          %mi d
                          %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  Q_{\Sigma_2} - Q_{\Sigma_1}
                  \&amp;=
                  \\int_{\Sigma_2} J - \int_{\Sigma_1} J
                  \\\
                  \&amp;=
                  \\int_{\partial X_4} J
                  \\\
                  \&amp; =
                  \\int_{X_4} d J
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            So if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            is closed, then total charge is conserved, while if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            is not closed, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d J
            is the local measure for how charge is being “created” or how it disappears.
          %p
            Now the remarkable fact is that in the standard model of particle physics,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            comes out non-closed. Its differetial instead comes out proportial to the 4-form which measures instanton number
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi J
                  %mo ∝
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d J \propto \langle F_\nabla \wedge F_\nabla \rangle
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is the famous
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chiral+anomaly"} chiral anomaly
          %p So far this is highlighted in every textbook. But the following further crucial subtlety tends not to be recognized for what it is.
          %p
            Namely on cosmological spacetimes that carry
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instantons"} instantons
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "Yang-Mills+instanton#FromTheMathsToThePhysicsStory"} Yang-Mills instanton – from the correct maths to the traditional physics story
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle F_\nabla \wedge F_\nabla\rangle
            is not in fact globally exact. The above formulas hold only locally, on a chart of spacetime. But on intersections two such pieces of data need to be glued by a gauge transformation. If we do make the usual simple assumptions (for simplicity of the discussion), then this gauge transformation is that famous “Chern-Simons winding number”
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 3
                    %mo →
                    %mi SU
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^3 \to SU(2)
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://physicsoverflow.org/38243/topological-number-integral-theory-boundary-volume-forms"} here
          %p
            As a result, in the presence of instantons, the integral of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle F_{\nabla} \wedge F_{\nabla}\rangle
            may be non-zero on a
            %em closed
            cosmological spacetime.
          %p
            The usual picture (which you see displayed in many popular accounts) is: imagine a 4d cup
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^4
            (a unit disk thought of as a “cup” cobordism from nothing to
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^3
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 3
                  %mo ×
                  %mi LongInterval
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^3 \times LongInterval
            and think of the result as being a simple model for the universe. Then assume some boundary condition saying that far in the future from the big bang the gauge fields
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            that carry those instantons decay away, hence are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vanishing+at+infinity"} vanishing at infinity
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^4
          %p
            So since the four form vanishes “at infinity”, we may just as well assume that it is supported on that cup
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^4
            in our model, the neighbourhood of the big bang.
          %p We learn that that:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The total net fermion charge in the universe is
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∫
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mn 4
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mo ∇
                        %mo ∧
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mo ∇
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℤ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_{S^4} \langle F_\nabla \wedge F_\nabla\rangle \in \mathbb{Z}
            %li
              %p
                this fermion number is picked up incrementally increasing from zero (at the origin of our
                = succeed "-cup," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi D
                          %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^4
                = succeed "\u201C" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \lt 1
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi t
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi D
                        %mi t
                        %mn 4
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 3
                  %msub
                    %mi J
                    %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Q_t
                  \=
                  \\int_{D^4_{t}} \langle F_\nabla \wedge F_\nabla\rangle
                  \=
                  \\int_{S^3} J_t
          %p
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi D
                    %mi t
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^4_t
            is the disk of radius
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \lt 1
          %p
            Then at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t = 1
            (in this paramneterization) the four form goes to zero (and in this fashion eventually extends to a global 4-form on our one-point compactified cosmological spacetime).
          %p
            So after the comoving time
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t = 1
            there is no net particle creation anymore. Moreover, the net particle number picked up until then is equal to the second Chern class of the cosmological gauge field, hence equal to the number that masure the “knottedness” of the cosmological gauge field (which we here think of as all being concentrated around the big big).
          %p One particle per knottedness of the cosmological gauge field.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/leptogenesis"} leptogenesis
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nucleosynthesis"} nucleosynthesis
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+Vortex+Atoms"} On Vortex Atoms
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comoving+time"} comoving time
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The original article stating the
            %em Sakharov conditions
            is
          %ul
            %li#Sakharov67
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrei+Sakharov"}> Andrei Sakharov
              ,
              = succeed "." do
                %em Violation of CP invariance, C asymmetry, and baryon asymmetry of the universe
              = succeed "." do
                %em Violation of CP invariance, C asymmetry, and baryon asymmetry of the universe
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1070/PU1991v034n05ABEH002497"} 10.1070/PU1991v034n05ABEH002497
          %p reviewed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dennis+Perepelitsa"}> Dennis Perepelitsa
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Sakharov Conditions for Baryogenesis
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://phys.columbia.edu/~dvp/dvp-sakharov.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The derivation that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instantons"} instantons
            lead to baryon number violation is due to
          %ul
            %li#Hooft76
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerard+%27t+Hooft"}> Gerard 't Hooft
                ,
                %em Symmetry Breaking through Bell-Jackiw Anomalies
                Phys. Rev. Lett. 37 (1976) (
                %a{:href => "http://www.staff.science.uu.nl/~hooft101/gthpub/symm_br_bell_jackiw.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerard+%27t+Hooft"} Gerard 't Hooft
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Computation of the quantum effects due to a four-dimensional pseudoparticle
          %p A good review of the axial anomaly is in
          %ul
            %li#Jackiw08
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roman+Jackiw"}> Roman Jackiw
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Axial anomaly
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.scholarpedia.org/article/Axial_anomaly"} web
          %p
            and of the resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
            of baryon number non-conservation is in
          %ul
            %li#Dine90
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Dine"}> Michael Dine
              ,
              %em Baryon number violation at high-energy in the standard model: Fact or fiction?
              Sep 1990, (
              %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/record/299206?ln=de"}> spire
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.slac.stanford.edu/cgi-wrap/getdoc/ssi90-020.pdf"} pdf
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Heidi Kuismanen, section 2 of
                %em Leptogenesis as the origin of matter-antimatter asymmetry in extra dimensionaL and supersymmetric modeLs
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/KuismanenBaryogenesis.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                V. A. Rubakov,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Shaposhnikov"} Mikhail Shaposhnikov
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Electroweak Baryon Number Non-Conservation in the Early Universe and in High Energy Collisions
                %strong 166
                (1996) 493-537 Phys. Usp. 39 (1996) 461-502 7lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/9603208"}> arXiv:hep-ph/9603208
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Antonio Riotto, Mark Trodden,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Recent Progress in Baryogenesis
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/9901362"} arXiv:hep-ph/9901362
            %li
              %p
                Laurent Canetti, Marco Drewes,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Shaposhnikov"} Mikhail Shaposhnikov
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Matter and Antimatter in the Universe
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.4186"} arXiv:1204.4186
            %li
              %p
                Yanagida,
                %em The origin of matter
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/YanagidaBaryogenesis.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#TroddenCarroll
              %p
                Mark Trodden, Sean Carroll, TASI Lectures: Introduction to Cosmology
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://ned.ipac.caltech.edu/level5/Sept03/Trodden/Trodden4_9.html"} 4.9 Baryon Number violation
            %li
              %p
                James M. Cline,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Baryogenesis
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/0609145"} arXiv:hep-ph/0609145
            %li#Shu08
              %p
                Jing Shu, section 7 of
                %em Connecting LHC Signals with Deep Physics at the TeV Scale and Baryogenesis
                2008
          %p
            Discussion with focus on
            = succeed "-effects:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
          %ul
            %li
              Dominik J. Schwarz,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The first second of the Universe
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0303574"} arXiv:astro-ph/0303574
        .revisedby
